    qa: [
      { q: 'Why are young pupils especially at risk on streets full of cars?', choices: ['They usually walk or cycle to school entirely on their own.', 'They are often driven to school by parents late for work.', 'They must cross neighboring streets that were once quiet.', 'They are small, easily distracted, and poor at judging speed.'], answer: 3, why: '第2段落：幼い児童は小さく、気が散りやすく、近づく車の速さを判断できないことがある。一人で歩く・自転車で通うのは閉鎖後に親が許せるようになること、親の遅刻や近くの通りの混雑は批判派の別の論点。' },
      { q: 'What happens in the walking groups organized by some schools?', choices: ['Volunteers at the barriers explain the rules to drivers.', 'An adult guides children from a meeting point on a safe route.', 'Parents park further away and walk with their own children.', 'Residents with permits drive children into the closed area.'], answer: 1, why: '第6段落：歩行グループでは大人が集合場所から安全な道を通って子どもの列を導く。柵のボランティアや住民の許可証は同じ段落の別の工夫、遠くに停めて歩くのは第4段落の批判派の指摘。' },
    ],
