    id: 'adv-boiling-pressure',
    title: 'Why Water Boils at Different Temperatures',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Most of us learn at school that water boils at one hundred degrees Celsius, and the number is so familiar that it seems to be a fixed property of water itself. In fact, it is true only under particular conditions, namely at the air pressure found near sea level. Climbers who cook on high mountains, and anyone who has used a pressure cooker in the kitchen, have experienced the surprising fact that the temperature at which water boils depends on how hard the surrounding air is pressing down on it.",
      "To understand why, it helps to think about what boiling actually is. Molecules in a pot of water are constantly moving, and some of those near the surface escape into the air as vapour even at room temperature, which is why a puddle slowly dries on a sunny afternoon. Boiling is different from this quiet evaporation, because it involves bubbles of vapour forming inside the liquid itself, and a bubble can only survive if the pressure of the vapour inside it is at least as great as the pressure pushing on it from outside.",
      "As water is heated, its vapour pressure rises, and boiling begins at the moment it equals the pressure of the atmosphere above the pot. At sea level this happens at about one hundred degrees, but on a high mountain, where the air is much thinner, the vapour needs less force to push back and the water boils at a lower temperature. On the summit of Mount Fuji, for example, water boils at roughly eighty-seven or eighty-eight degrees, and near the top of Everest at only about seventy degrees.",
      "This has consequences for anyone trying to cook at a high altitude. Water that bubbles violently may look extremely hot, but once it is boiling it cannot get any hotter, ( A ) how much heat is supplied, because the extra energy is used to turn liquid into vapour rather than to raise the temperature. On a mountain, therefore, rice may remain hard and noodles take much longer to soften, since the food is being cooked in water that is well below the temperature found in an ordinary kitchen.",
      "A pressure cooker uses the same principle in the opposite direction. Its tightly sealed lid traps the steam produced inside, so that the pressure in the pot rises well above that of the surrounding air, and the water must reach a higher temperature, often around one hundred and twenty degrees, before it can boil. Because the chemical changes that soften food generally proceed faster at higher temperatures, tough meat and dried beans that might need hours in an open pot can become tender in a fraction of the time, which also saves fuel.",
      "The same relationship between pressure and boiling appears in many other places, from the cooling systems of car engines, which are kept under pressure so that the liquid inside does not boil away, to the machines used in hospitals to kill bacteria with steam hotter than one hundred degrees. Once we recognize that the familiar number learned at school describes a balance between a liquid and the air above it, rather than a fixed rule, ordinary experiences such as a slow meal on a camping trip begin to make scientific sense.",
    ],
