    id: 'adv-dark-patterns',
    title: 'When Design Works Against Us',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Anyone who has tried to cancel an online subscription has probably discovered that leaving can be far harder than joining. Signing up may take a single click, but cancelling may require searching through several menus, answering a series of questions, and finally telephoning a customer service center that is open only during working hours. Such experiences are rarely accidental, and designers and researchers now have a name for them: dark patterns, a term introduced around 2010 by the British user-experience designer Harry Brignull to describe features of websites and apps that are deliberately designed to lead users into decisions they would not otherwise make.",
      "Dark patterns take many forms, and some are so common that we hardly notice them. A box agreeing to receive advertising emails may already be ticked when we reach the end of a form, a countdown timer may warn us that a special price will disappear in minutes even though the same offer returns the next day, and a button for declining an offer may be written in words designed to make us feel foolish, such as “No thanks, I prefer paying full price.” In each case, the design takes advantage of the fact that people are often busy, distracted, or anxious, and that they tend to accept whatever choice requires the least effort.",
      "Defenders of these techniques sometimes argue that they are simply a modern version of ordinary salesmanship, no different from a shopkeeper who places sweets near the checkout or a salesperson who speaks enthusiastically about a product. There is some truth in this comparison, ( A ) the digital versions differ in at least two important ways. First, they can be tested on millions of users and adjusted continuously until they produce the greatest possible effect, and second, they operate inside systems that collect detailed information about each person, allowing the pressure to be targeted at the moments when individuals are most likely to give in.",
      "The ethical problem with dark patterns is not merely that they cost people money, though they often do, but that they quietly undermine the idea that a choice belongs to the person who makes it. When a decision is shaped by confusion, hidden information, or artificial urgency, it no longer reflects what the user actually wants, and yet the company can still claim that the customer freely agreed. Over time, such practices may also damage trust more widely, as people who feel tricked by one service become suspicious of digital services in general, including those that are designed honestly.",
      "Governments have begun to respond to these concerns in a variety of ways. In the European Union, recent laws on digital services and consumer protection prohibit certain manipulative designs, and in the United States the Federal Trade Commission has taken action against companies that made cancelling subscriptions unreasonably difficult. Regulation faces real difficulties, however, because the line between persuasion and manipulation is not always clear, and because designers can invent new techniques faster than lawmakers can describe and ban the old ones.",
      "For this reason, many experts believe that laws must be accompanied by changes within the design profession itself and by greater awareness among users. Some designers now argue for a principle of fairness, according to which leaving a service should be as easy as joining it and the option that benefits the company should never be made more attractive simply by hiding the alternatives. Users, for their part, can learn to recognize common tricks, to slow down when a website insists that they hurry, and to remember that a well-designed system should help them do what they intend rather than steer them toward what someone else wants.",
    ],
