    id: 'adv-snoring',
    title: 'Why Do People Snore?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Almost everyone has heard someone snoring at night, whether it is a family member at home, a friend on a school trip, or a stranger on a long train ride. Snoring can be funny to talk about the next morning, but for the people who share a room with a loud snorer, it can be a serious problem that keeps them awake for hours. To understand why snoring happens, we need to look at what goes on inside the body while we are asleep.",
      "When we breathe, air passes through the nose and mouth and then goes down a tube in the throat to the lungs. During the day, the muscles around this tube keep it open, so the air can move freely. When we fall asleep, however, these muscles relax, and the soft parts at the back of the throat become loose. If the passage becomes narrow, the air moving through it makes these soft parts shake, and this shaking produces the sound that we call snoring.",
      "Several things make snoring more likely. People who sleep on their backs often snore more, because the tongue can fall backward and block part of the throat. A stuffy nose caused by a cold or an allergy can also force people to breathe through their mouths, which usually makes snoring worse. Being overweight, drinking alcohol before bed, and simply getting older are other common causes, since each of them can make the throat narrower or its muscles weaker.",
      "For most people, snoring is only a noise that bothers others in the room. ( A ), in some cases it can be a sign of a health problem that needs attention. Some people stop breathing for a few seconds again and again during the night, and then wake up a little in order to start breathing again. Because their sleep is broken so many times, they often feel very tired during the day, even after spending eight hours in bed. Doctors say that people who snore loudly and also feel sleepy in the daytime should get a medical check.",
      "The good news is that there are simple ways to reduce snoring. Sleeping on one side instead of on the back, keeping the air in the bedroom from becoming too dry, and avoiding alcohol in the evening can all help a great deal. Some people also use special pillows or small strips that open the nose a little wider while they sleep. Snoring may seem like a small problem, but paying attention to it can lead to better sleep both for the snorer and for everyone nearby.",
    ],
