    id: 'adv-shopping-cart',
    title: 'The Basket on Wheels',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "When we go to a supermarket today, one of the first things we do is take a shopping cart. It seems natural to push a large metal basket on wheels around the store, filling it with food and other goods as we go. Yet the shopping cart is a fairly recent invention, and when it first appeared, many shoppers did not want to use it at all.",
      "Before the 1930s, most people carried a small basket in their hands while they shopped. Because a heavy basket quickly became tiring, customers usually bought only what they could hold, and store owners knew that this limited how much they were able to sell. In 1937, a grocery store owner in Oklahoma City in the United States named Sylvan Goldman began to think about how this problem might be solved.",
      "His idea was simple. He placed two baskets on a folding chair and added wheels to its legs, so that shoppers could push their goods instead of carrying them. Goldman expected customers to be delighted, but at first almost nobody used the new carts. Many men felt that pushing a cart made them look weak, and many women said it reminded them of pushing a baby carriage, which they had already done for years.",
      "Goldman did not give up. He hired men and women to walk around his store pushing carts and pretending to shop, and he placed a worker at the entrance to offer a cart to everyone who came in. Seeing other people use the carts made new customers feel more comfortable, and before long the carts were being used by almost every shopper in the store.",
      "( A ) the carts became common, customers began to buy far more at each visit, because they no longer had to worry about the weight of their goods. Stores became larger, and their shelves were filled with a much wider variety of products. Later designs allowed carts to fit inside one another, which saved space, and today carts come in many sizes, including small ones for children and ones with seats for babies.",
      "The history of the shopping cart shows that a good invention is not always accepted right away. People are often slow to change their habits, especially when a new tool makes them feel uncomfortable in front of others. Sometimes an inventor must change not only the object itself but also the way that people think about using it in their daily lives.",
    ],
