      "If you stand on a busy train platform in Tokyo, you may hear a short, cheerful tune just before the doors close. These tunes, known as departure melodies, last only a few seconds, but they have become one of the most familiar sounds of daily life in Japanese cities. Visitors from abroad are often surprised by them, because in many other countries trains leave with nothing more than a whistle, a beep, or a recorded voice.",
      "For many years, Japanese stations used loud electric bells to tell passengers that a train was about to leave. The bells did their job, but many people found them harsh and stressful, especially at crowded stations where several platforms rang at almost the same time. Around 1989, some large stations in Tokyo began to replace the bells with short pieces of music that were designed to be clear but gentle, and the idea soon spread to other lines and other cities.",
      "Each melody has to meet some practical conditions. It must be loud enough to be heard over the noise of a large crowd, ( A ) it must not be so loud or so sharp that it makes people nervous. It also has to end at the right moment, so that passengers understand that the doors are about to close and do not try to rush onto the train. Some composers say that a good melody should make people feel ready to stop, not ready to run.",
      "Over time, many stations have chosen melodies that are connected to their own neighborhood. Takadanobaba Station in Tokyo, for example, plays the theme song of the cartoon Astro Boy, because the company started by the artist who created the character is located in the area. Other stations use local folk songs or songs about nearby mountains and rivers. For people who live there, these tunes have become a small part of the identity of their town, and some travelers even visit stations just to listen to them.",
