    paragraphs: [
      "When people open a bottle of wine, they rarely think about the small cork that has kept the drink safe inside. Yet this simple object comes from a remarkable tree called the cork oak, which grows mainly in Portugal, Spain, and other countries around the western Mediterranean Sea. What makes the tree special is that its thick outer bark can be removed without killing it, and after some years the bark slowly grows back again.",
      "The bark of the cork oak is made of millions of tiny cells filled with air. Because of this structure, cork is very light, it floats on water, and it does not let liquids or gases pass through easily. It is also soft and elastic, so a piece of cork that has been pressed into a narrow bottle neck slowly returns to its original size and fits tightly. These qualities explain why people have used cork for thousands of years.",
      "Harvesting cork is skilled work that is still done by hand. Workers use a special axe to cut the bark carefully and then pull it off the trunk in large pieces, taking care not to damage the living layer underneath. A tree must be about twenty-five years old before its bark can be harvested for the first time, and after that it can be stripped only once every nine years. ( A ), a single tree, which may live for more than two hundred years, can be harvested around fifteen times during its long life.",
      "Cork forests are also important for nature. Because the trees are not cut down, the forests can remain in place for centuries and provide homes for many kinds of animals and plants, including rare birds such as the Spanish imperial eagle. The forests also help protect the soil from drying out and turning into desert, which is a serious danger in the hot, dry summers of southern Europe.",
      "In recent decades, however, the cork industry has faced a challenge. Many wine makers began to use plastic stoppers and metal screw caps, which are cheaper and never give the wine an unpleasant taste, a problem that sometimes happens with natural cork. If demand for cork falls, farmers may earn less money from their trees, and some may decide to replace their cork forests with other crops or simply leave them without care.",
      "To protect the forests, producers are now looking for new uses for cork. Today it is used in floors, walls, shoes, and even parts of spacecraft, because it is light and keeps heat out well. Some people say that choosing products made from cork is a small but useful way to support a traditional industry and the rich natural environment that depends on it.",
    ],
