    paragraphs: [
      "For most people, the letter A is simply a shape on a page, and the number seven is just a way of counting things. For some people, however, every letter and number appears with its own color, as clearly as the black ink in which it is printed. To them, A might always be red, B might be a soft blue, and seven might be a deep green. This experience is called synesthesia, a word that comes from Greek and means something like \"sensing together.\" It is not an illness, and many people who have it say that they cannot imagine seeing the world in any other way.",
      "Synesthesia comes in many different forms. Seeing colors with letters and numbers is one of the most common types, but there are others that sound even more surprising. Some people see shapes or colors when they listen to music, and a few report that certain words have a taste in their mouth. Scientists once believed that the condition was extremely rare, but larger surveys now suggest that around four in every hundred people may have some type of it. Many of them do not realize that they are unusual until they mention their colors to a friend and are met with a puzzled look.",
      "At first, some researchers doubted that these experiences were real. They wondered whether people with synesthesia were simply remembering the colors of alphabet toys they had played with as children. To test this idea, scientists showed volunteers the same letters several months apart and asked them to name the color of each one. People with synesthesia chose almost exactly the same colors each time, even though they had no warning that they would be tested again. ( A ), people without synesthesia, who were asked to invent colors and remember them, gave far less consistent answers.",
      "Why does this happen? One popular idea is that the parts of the brain which handle letters and colors lie close to each other, and that in some people they are more strongly connected than usual. Young children's brains have a great many connections between different areas, and most of these are removed as the children grow older. In people with synesthesia, some of these early connections may remain. The condition also tends to run in families, which suggests that genes play a part, although members of the same family often see quite different colors for the same letter.",
      "For many people with synesthesia, their colors are a quiet help in daily life. Some say that the colors make it easier for them to remember phone numbers, names, or the spelling of difficult words, because a mistake simply looks wrong to them. Several well-known artists and musicians have described their own synesthesia and used it in their work. Studying the condition also helps scientists understand how all of our brains combine information from different senses. In this way, a small group of people who see colorful letters may teach the rest of us a great deal about how ordinary seeing and hearing work.",
    ],
