    id: 'adv-simpson-paradox',
    title: 'When the Whole Disagrees with Its Parts',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Imagine reading a report about two treatments for the same illness. In a group of patients with mild cases, the first treatment works better than the second, and in a group with severe cases, the first treatment again works better. It would seem obvious that the first treatment must also be better when all the patients are counted together. Surprisingly, this is not guaranteed. It is entirely possible for one option to win in every single group and yet lose when the groups are combined, a pattern that statisticians call Simpson's paradox.",
      "A well-known medical study from the 1980s shows how this can happen. Doctors compared two ways of removing kidney stones: traditional open surgery and a newer, less invasive method. For small stones, open surgery succeeded in about 93 percent of cases, compared with 87 percent for the newer method. For large stones, open surgery again did better, with a success rate of about 73 percent against 69 percent. When all the cases were added together, however, the newer method appeared to be the winner, succeeding in 83 percent of cases while open surgery succeeded in only 78 percent.",
      "The explanation lies in how the patients were divided. Doctors tended to choose open surgery for the large, difficult stones and the newer method for the small, easy ones. Because large stones are harder to treat whatever method is used, open surgery was weighed down by a heavy share of difficult cases, while the newer method was helped by a large share of easy ones. The overall figures therefore reflected not only the quality of each treatment but also the kind of patients each one happened to receive, and this second factor was strong enough to reverse the comparison.",
      "The same pattern appeared in a famous case involving university admissions. In the early 1970s, figures from one American graduate school seemed to show that men were admitted at a noticeably higher rate than women, and the school was worried that it might be accused of unfair treatment. ( A ), when researchers looked at each department separately, they found that women were admitted at a similar or even higher rate in most of them. The difference arose because women had applied in greater numbers to the departments that accepted only a small proportion of all their applicants, while men had more often applied to departments that were easier to enter.",
      "It might be tempting to conclude that the figures for separate groups are always more trustworthy than the combined figures, but this would be a mistake. Whether we should split the data or combine it depends on how the factors are related to each other, and that cannot be read from the numbers alone. In the kidney stone case, the size of the stone existed before the treatment was chosen and influenced that choice, so comparing patients with similar stones makes sense. If, on the other hand, the grouping factor were itself a result of the treatment, splitting the data could hide the very effect we wanted to measure.",
      "This is why statisticians increasingly insist that data cannot speak for itself. To decide which comparison is fair, we need some understanding of the process that produced the numbers: who chose what, for what reasons, and in what order events occurred. Two analysts working with exactly the same table may reach opposite conclusions if they hold different beliefs about these causes, and neither can settle the argument simply by calculating more carefully. The numbers tell us what happened, but only a story about causes can tell us why.",
      "For ordinary readers, Simpson's paradox offers a useful habit of mind. Whenever a news report compares two hospitals, schools, or policies using a single overall figure, it is worth asking whether the groups being compared faced the same conditions. A hospital that treats the most serious cases may have a worse survival rate than its neighbor even if it is better at every type of operation. A single number can be perfectly accurate and still give a misleading impression, and learning to look beneath it is one of the most valuable lessons that statistics can teach.",
    ],
