    id: 'adv-city-river-swimming',
    title: 'Swimming Back into City Rivers',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "For most of the twentieth century, the idea of swimming in the river that runs through a large city would have seemed either brave or foolish. Factories poured waste into the water, sewers emptied directly into it, and in many places swimming was officially banned. In Paris, for instance, swimming in the Seine was prohibited in 1923, and for the next hundred years the river was something to admire from its bridges rather than something to enter.",
      "In recent years, however, a growing number of cities have begun to invite their residents back into the water. Paris spent about 1.4 billion euros on improving its water system before the 2024 Olympic Games, when athletes swam in the Seine during the triathlon and the open-water races, and in the summer of 2025 the city opened several public swimming areas in the river for the first time in a century. Copenhagen had already shown what was possible in 2002, when it opened a free harbor bath in an area that had once been a busy industrial port.",
      "Behind these changes lies a long and expensive effort to clean the water. The most difficult problem in many old cities is a design in which rainwater and sewage flow through the same underground pipes. On dry days, this mixture is carried safely to treatment plants, but during heavy rain the pipes overflow, and untreated waste pours straight into the river. To prevent this, cities have built huge underground tanks that store the extra water until the treatment plants are ready to deal with it.",
      "Even with such improvements, swimming in a city river is never completely free of risk. After a storm, the level of bacteria in the water can rise quickly, and swimming areas must sometimes close for several days at a time, which happened repeatedly during the Paris Olympics. Strong currents and passing boats create further dangers, so most cities allow swimming only in marked areas with lifeguards, and they publish daily reports on water quality so that people can decide for themselves whether to go in.",
      "Some cities, ( A ), have never really stopped swimming in their rivers. In Swiss cities such as Basel and Zurich, people have long floated down the Rhine and the Limmat on summer evenings after work, carrying their clothes and phones in special waterproof bags. These traditions show that a river can be both a working waterway and a place for everyday pleasure, as long as the water is kept clean and people understand and respect the rules.",
      "Supporters argue that the benefits go far beyond a cool swim on a hot afternoon. As summers grow hotter, rivers can offer free places for people to cool down, especially those who cannot afford air conditioning or trips to the beach. Perhaps more importantly, when residents swim in a river, they begin to care about what goes into it, and a city that once turned its back on its water may come to see it as a shared public space that deserves protection.",
    ],
