    id: 'adv-tonle-sap-lake',
    title: 'The Lake That Breathes: Cambodia\'s Reversing River',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Rivers, as everyone learns at school, flow in one direction, from higher ground toward the sea. Yet in Cambodia there is a river that changes direction twice a year, and its unusual behavior supports one of the richest freshwater ecosystems on Earth. The Tonle Sap River connects the Mekong, one of the great rivers of Asia, to the Tonle Sap Lake, the largest body of fresh water in Southeast Asia, and for several months each year it carries water not out of the lake but into it.",
      "The cause of this remarkable reversal is the monsoon. During the dry season, from roughly November to May, the lake is shallow, often only about a meter deep, and its water drains slowly southward through the river into the Mekong. When the summer rains arrive and snow melts in the mountains far upstream, however, the level of the Mekong rises so much that it becomes higher than the lake. Water then pushes back up the Tonle Sap River, and the lake swells to several times its dry-season size, spreading across forests and fields and reaching a depth of up to about ten meters in places.",
      "This annual flood, which scientists sometimes call a flood pulse, is the engine of the lake's extraordinary productivity. As the water spreads into the surrounding forests, fish move into the newly flooded areas to feed on insects, seeds, and decaying plants, and many species lay their eggs there. When the water retreats in the autumn, the young fish are carried back toward the lake and the Mekong, having grown rapidly during the months of plenty. The result is one of the largest inland fisheries in the world, and fish from the lake and its connecting rivers have long supplied a large share of the animal protein in the Cambodian diet.",
      "Human life around the lake has adapted closely to the rhythm of the water. Many families live in floating villages, where houses, schools, and even shops rest on rafts or boats and are moved as the shoreline advances and retreats. Others build their homes on tall wooden stilts so that the floodwater can pass beneath them, and farmers plant rice on land that has been enriched by the sediment left behind by the flood. Every November, when the river resumes its normal course, Cambodians celebrate the change with a water festival that includes boat races in the capital, Phnom Penh.",
      "In recent years, however, scientists have become worried that this delicate system may be weakening. Dozens of hydroelectric dams have been built on the Mekong and its branches upstream, in China and Laos, and these dams store water during the wet season and release it during the dry season. Such a pattern helps to produce electricity and to reduce flooding downstream, but it also smooths out the very differences in water level that ( A ) the reversal of the Tonle Sap River. In 2019 and 2020, during a period of unusually low rainfall, the reversal came late and was much weaker than usual, and fishing communities reported sharply reduced catches.",
      "Climate change adds further uncertainty, since shifts in the timing and strength of the monsoon could alter the flood pulse in ways that are difficult to predict. Meanwhile, the flooded forests around the lake have been cleared in many places for farmland and firewood, removing the very habitats where young fish feed and grow. Because these pressures act together, it is often hard to say how much any single factor is responsible for a poor fishing season, and this uncertainty makes it difficult for the governments of the region to agree on who should change their behavior.",
      "The story of the Tonle Sap is a reminder that the value of a river cannot be measured only by the amount of water it carries or the electricity it can generate. Its timing, its rhythms, and its seasonal extremes are part of what makes it useful to the people and creatures that depend on it. Protecting such a system requires cooperation across national borders, because decisions made in one country, hundreds of kilometers upstream, can determine whether families in another country have enough fish to eat.",
    ],
