    id: 'kyodai-on-lids',
    title: 'On Lids',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Of all the objects in an ordinary kitchen, the lid is perhaps the one we notice least, because it does its work by being put on and taken off, and it seldom attracts attention unless it has gone missing. We tend to regard it as a mere accessory to the container, but without it most containers would be far less useful than they appear on the shelf. Yet a lid performs a surprisingly delicate task. It divides the world into an inside and an outside without permanently separating them, and it allows us to decide, again and again, the moment at which the two should meet.",
      "Consider the lid of a cooking pot. When it is in place, heat and moisture are kept within, and the soup simmers quietly in a small, enclosed climate of its own making. When it is lifted, steam rushes out, the smell fills the room, and the cook is able to look, taste, and adjust. A good cook knows that the lid is not simply a cover but an instrument of timing, and that a dish can be ruined as easily by lifting it too often as by forgetting to lift it at all.",
      "There is also a particular pleasure in lifting a lid whose contents we cannot see. A child opening a box at a birthday party, a traveler lifting the cover of a dish in a foreign restaurant, and an old woman opening a tin of buttons that she has not touched in years all share a moment of suspended expectation. The lid, in such cases, does more than protect what lies beneath it; it creates the small interval of not yet knowing, and that interval is often more memorable than whatever is finally revealed.",
      "( A ), not every lid opens willingly. Most of us have struggled with a jar that refuses to turn, twisting until our palms ache and finally handing it to someone else, who opens it at once and with a faintly embarrassing ease. The stubborn jar is a modest lesson in dependence, for it reminds us that some things are opened not by greater effort but by a different pair of hands, or by the simple trick of tapping the edge or warming the metal under running water.",
      "Our language has borrowed the lid for the management of feeling. We speak of keeping a lid on anger, of putting a lid on a scandal, and of a situation in which the lid finally blows off, and in each phrase the emotion is imagined as something hot that rises and presses against a cover. The image is revealing, because it suggests that we rarely expect feelings to disappear; at most we hope to contain them until a safer moment arrives, much as a cook holds the steam until the dish is ready.",
      "Closing a lid can also mark an ending. People close the lid of a piano when the last note has faded, close the lid of a laptop when the working day is over, and, in many cultures, close the lid of a coffin as the final gesture of farewell. In each case the lid does not destroy what it covers, yet it declares that the time for looking and touching has passed, and the soft sound it makes as it settles often carries more meaning than any words that could be spoken at that point.",
      "Perhaps this is why we are so oddly troubled by the drawer full of containers whose lids have been lost, or by the lid that fits nothing we still own. A container without its lid can hold things but cannot keep them, and a lid without its container has nothing left to protect or to reveal. Lids remind us that much of ordinary life consists not in having or losing things but in choosing when to open and when to close, and that the quiet skill of timing those choices well is one we practice every day without ever naming it.",
    ],
