    title: 'How the Iberian Lynx Came Back',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "The Iberian lynx is a wild cat about twice the size of a pet cat, with long legs, a short tail, and tufts of black hair on the tips of its ears. It lives only in Spain and Portugal, and at the beginning of this century it was in serious danger of disappearing. In 2002, scientists counted fewer than one hundred of these animals, living in two small areas of southern Spain, and many experts believed that the species would become the first wild cat to die out in thousands of years.",
      "The main reason for this sharp fall was a lack of food. The lynx eats mostly rabbits, and in some places rabbits make up almost nine tenths of its diet. During the twentieth century, two diseases spread among European rabbits and killed huge numbers of them, so the lynx suddenly found it very hard to feed itself and its young. At the same time, forests and bushland were cleared for farms and roads, many lynx were killed by cars, and others were caught in traps that had been set for other animals.",
      "To save the species, the governments of Spain and Portugal, scientists, and nature groups started working together. They built special centers where lynx could be bred safely, and the first cubs were born there in 2005. Young cats raised in these centers were later released into areas where the lynx had once lived, including parts of Portugal where it had not been seen for many years. Workers also put rabbits back into the countryside and planted bushes where the rabbits could hide and raise their own young.",
      "Another important step was to win the support of the people who owned the land. Most of the places where the lynx could live were private farms and hunting estates, so the project needed the help of farmers and hunters. ( A ), many of them had seen the lynx as a danger to their animals, but they began to welcome it when they learned that it also kept away foxes, which often attacked chickens and young rabbits. Some roads were given tunnels so that the cats could cross safely, and drivers were warned with signs where lynx were often seen.",
      "The results have been remarkable. By 2023 the number of Iberian lynx had risen to more than two thousand, and in 2024 the species was moved from the list of endangered animals to the less serious group called vulnerable. The lynx is not yet completely safe, because new diseases, traffic, and very hot summers could still harm it. Even so, its story shows that a species close to the edge can recover when food, safe places to live, and the support of local people are all provided at the same time.",
    ],
