    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "If you walk through a park in London on a winter morning, you may hear loud, high calls and see a group of bright green birds flying over the trees. These are rose-ringed parakeets, a kind of small parrot with a long tail and a red beak. Their natural home is in parts of Africa and South Asia, far from Europe, but today they live in large numbers in many European cities and have even become a familiar sight in some parts of Tokyo.",
      "Nobody knows exactly how the parakeets first arrived in London, and several popular stories have been told about them. One says that a famous rock musician let a pair of birds go in the city in the late 1960s. Another says that they escaped from a film studio where a movie set in Africa was being made. In 2019, however, a group of scientists studied old records of where the birds had been seen and found no evidence for these stories. The birds most likely came from many different pet owners who released them or lost them over a long period.",
      "Many people were surprised that a bird from a warm climate could survive the cold winters of northern Europe. In fact, the rose-ringed parakeet naturally lives in some areas, such as the foothills of the Himalayas, where winters can also be cool. In cities, it also gets a great deal of help from people. Many homes have feeders filled with seeds and nuts for small garden birds, and the parakeets have learned to visit them, especially in winter when other food is hard to find.",
      "The number of parakeets has grown quickly, and estimates suggest that there are now tens of thousands of them in and around London alone. Some people enjoy their bright colors and lively behavior, which add a touch of the tropics to grey city streets. ( A ), other people are worried about them. The birds can be very noisy when they gather in large groups in the evening, and they sometimes eat fruit in gardens and on farms near the cities where they live.",
      "Scientists are also studying how the parakeets affect native birds. Parakeets build their nests in holes in old trees, the same places that some local birds need for raising their young. Because the parakeets start nesting early in the year, they may take the best holes before the native birds are ready. So far, the effects seem to be smaller than some people feared, but researchers continue to watch closely, because the story of these green birds shows how human choices can change the wildlife that lives right outside our windows.",
    ],
