    id: 'adv-rope-festival-kawanobe',
    title: 'A Straw Rope That Holds a Town Together',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "In the small river town of Kawanobe, which exists only in this story but resembles many real places in rural Japan, the most important day of the year is not a national holiday. It is the second Sunday of January, when the whole town gathers on a frozen field to pull a giant rope made of rice straw. The event is called the Rope Festival, and people in the town say that it has been held every winter for more than three hundred years.",
      "The rope is not bought from a shop; it is made by the residents themselves. In early December, families bring bundles of straw from the autumn harvest to the community hall, and for about two weeks people of all ages twist the straw into thin cords. The cords are then braided together until the finished rope is about forty meters long and as thick as an adult's body, so heavy that dozens of people are needed just to carry it to the field.",
      "On the day of the festival, the town is divided into two teams: the families who live on the hills and the families who live near the river. According to old tradition, a victory for the hill team means a good rice harvest, while a victory for the river team means plenty of fish. Because both results bring something good, nobody truly loses, and the losing team usually laughs as loudly as the winners when the rope finally slides across the line.",
      "In recent years, however, the festival has faced serious difficulties. Most farmers now use large machines that cut the straw into small pieces during the harvest, so long straw suitable for making rope has become hard to find. ( A ), the number of young people in the town has fallen, and in some years there were barely enough people to finish the rope before the festival day.",
      "The town found creative ways to keep the tradition alive. A few farmers agreed to harvest one field by hand every year only for the festival, and the local elementary school began teaching children how to twist straw as part of its classes. University students from a nearby city now visit in December to help, and some of them return every year because they enjoy the warm welcome they receive from the older residents.",
      "After the festival, the rope is not thrown away. It is cut into pieces and spread over the fields, where it slowly breaks down and returns nutrients to the soil for the next crop. In this way, the Rope Festival connects the end of one harvest with the beginning of another, and it reminds the people of Kawanobe that their town, like the rope itself, is strongest when many small threads are twisted together.",
    ],
    ja: [
      '川辺の小さな町、川野辺は、この話の中にしか存在しないが、日本の地方の多くの実在の場所によく似ている。この町で一年でいちばん大切な日は国民の祝日ではない。それは1月の第2日曜日で、町じゅうの人が凍った田んぼに集まり、稲わらで作った巨大な綱を引く日である。この行事は綱祭りと呼ばれ、町の人々は、300年以上前から毎年冬に行われてきたと言う。',
      '綱は店で買うものではなく、住民自身が作る。12月の初めに、各家庭が秋の収穫で出たわらの束を公民館に持ち寄り、およそ2週間、あらゆる年代の人がわらをより合わせて細いなわにする。そのなわをさらに編み合わせ、完成した綱は長さ約40メートル、太さは大人の体ほどになり、会場の田んぼまで運ぶだけでも何十人もの人が必要なほど重い。',
      '祭りの当日、町は二つの組に分かれる。丘の上に住む家族と、川の近くに住む家族である。古い言い伝えによれば、丘の組が勝てば米が豊作になり、川の組が勝てば魚がたくさんとれるという。どちらの結果もよいことをもたらすので、本当に負ける人はおらず、綱がついに線をこえると、負けた組もたいてい勝った組と同じくらい大声で笑う。',
      'しかし近年、祭りは深刻な困難に直面している。今ではほとんどの農家が、収穫の際にわらを細かく切ってしまう大型の機械を使うため、綱作りに向いた長いわらが手に入りにくくなった。それに加えて、町の若者の数が減り、祭りの日までに綱を仕上げる人手がやっと足りるかどうかという年もあった。',
      '町は伝統を守るためにさまざまなくふうをした。何軒かの農家が、祭りのためだけに毎年一枚の田を手で刈り取ることを引き受け、地元の小学校は授業の一部として子どもたちにわらのより方を教えはじめた。今では近くの都市の大学生が12月に手伝いに来ており、その中には、年配の住民から受ける温かいもてなしが楽しくて毎年やって来る学生もいる。',
      '祭りが終わっても、綱は捨てられない。細かく切られて田畑にまかれ、そこでゆっくりと分解されて、次の作物のために土に栄養を返す。このように綱祭りは、一つの収穫の終わりを次の収穫の始まりへとつなぎ、町が綱そのものと同じように、たくさんの細い糸がより合わされたときにもっとも強くなるということを、川野辺の人々に思い出させているのである。',
    ],
