    id: 'adv-bell-free-week',
    title: 'A Week Without School Bells',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "At most schools in Japan, a bell or chime tells students when each lesson begins and ends, and few people ever stop to ask whether it is really necessary. Last year, however, the teachers at Midori Hills Junior High School, a school with about four hundred students, decided to find out what would happen if the bells were turned off for one whole week. Their aim was to see whether students could learn to manage their own time without being told when to move.",
      "Before the experiment began, the teachers explained the plan to the students and placed large clocks in every classroom and hallway. Students were told that they were responsible for arriving at their next class on time, and teachers agreed to end each lesson by watching the clock instead of waiting for the bell. Some parents worried that the change would cause confusion, but the school promised to return to the usual system if serious problems appeared.",
      "On the first two days, the results were not very encouraging. A number of students arrived late after lunch because they were talking with friends and forgot to check the time, and a few teachers continued their lessons for several minutes after the period had ended. ( A ), by the end of the week the number of late students had fallen below the level of a normal week with bells, and teachers were finishing their lessons on time.",
      "The teachers also noticed other changes that they had not expected. The hallways became calmer between lessons, because students no longer rushed out of their classrooms all at once as soon as the bell rang. In a survey, several students said that they felt more grown-up, and one student wrote that she had started to plan her free time more carefully because nobody was going to remind her. On the other hand, some students said that they felt nervous about being late and kept looking at the clock during their lessons.",
      "After the week was over, the teachers and students discussed the results together in each class. They decided to keep the bell only at the beginning and the end of the school day and to turn it off for the short breaks between lessons. The principal explained that the experiment had been useful not because it gave a perfect answer but because it made everyone think about a habit that they had always accepted without question.",
    ],
