    id: 'kyodai-on-combs',
    title: 'On Combs',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Of all the objects that we carry with us or keep within easy reach, the comb may be the one that has changed least over the centuries. A row of narrow teeth fixed to a spine was already being cut from bone and wood many thousands of years ago, and the plastic comb lying at the back of a bathroom drawer today would be instantly recognizable to someone from almost any period of human history. Few tools have had such a long and uneventful career, and perhaps for that very reason we hardly ever think about the comb, even though most of us pick one up every morning without a moment's hesitation.",
      "What a comb does is to impose order on something that tends naturally towards disorder. Hair left to itself becomes tangled, not because of any fault in its owner but simply because thousands of fine strands moving freely against one another will always find ways to knot together. The comb does not fight this tendency with force; it works patiently from the ends towards the roots, separating one small section at a time, and anyone who has tried to drag it through a knot all at once knows how painful and useless that approach can be. There is a modest lesson here about how order is usually achieved, ( A ) through gradual and repeated attention rather than through a single decisive effort.",
      "It is worth noticing, too, that a comb is defined as much by its gaps as by its teeth. The spaces between the teeth determine what passes through and what is held back, and a comb with wide spaces serves quite a different purpose from one with narrow spaces, which catches finer things and gives a smoother result. In this sense the comb resembles many human institutions, such as examinations, rules and customs, whose character depends less on what they contain than on what they allow to slip through. A society, like a comb, may reveal its nature most clearly in the size of the gaps that it chooses to leave.",
      "The comb is also an unusually personal object. People lend pens, books and even clothes without much thought, but many feel uneasy about sharing a comb, as if something of the owner remained among its teeth after use. This instinct is partly a matter of hygiene, but it seems to express something deeper as well. Hair has long been treated in many cultures as a part of the self that can be separated from the body and kept as a memento of a loved one or a child, and the comb, which touches it every day, seems to share a little of that intimacy, becoming a kind of silent witness to our private routines.",
      "At the same time, the comb belongs to the public side of life, since its purpose is to prepare us to be seen. The few seconds spent combing one's hair before leaving the house mark a small transition from the private self, unguarded and untidy, to the self that is presented to others. Rituals of this kind are easy to dismiss as vanity, but they may be better understood as a form of courtesy, an acknowledgment that other people will have to look at us and that we owe them at least a moment of preparation, however ordinary the day ahead may turn out to be.",
      "Combs also wear out in a quiet and revealing way. Their teeth break one by one, usually without anyone noticing exactly when, and a comb may remain in use for years with gaps that grow gradually wider. It is often only when a new one is bought that the old one is seen clearly for what it has become. Many of the things on which we depend, from friendships to habits of mind, decline in the same unnoticed manner, continuing to serve us adequately until a sudden comparison shows how much they have changed while we were not paying attention.",
      "None of this is to claim that the comb conceals some profound philosophy. It is a cheap and humble tool, and its usefulness lies precisely in the fact that it asks for no attention at all. Yet objects that have accompanied human beings for so long tend to absorb something of our habits and values, and to look closely at them is to see ourselves from an unexpected angle. The comb, drawn patiently through a tangle each morning, offers a small picture of how we try, day after day, to bring some order to lives that never stay neatly arranged for very long.",
    ],
