      "Every summer, the small town of Minamikawa holds an unusual event on the river that runs through its center. One thousand yellow plastic ducks, each with a number painted on its back, are dropped into the water from a bridge at the same moment. They float slowly down to a finish line about five hundred meters away, and the people who bought the first three ducks to arrive receive prizes from local shops.",
      "The duck race began twelve years ago as a way to raise money for the children's ward of the town hospital. Each duck costs five hundred yen to adopt, and families often buy several so that every child can cheer for a duck of his or her own. Last year, the race raised more than four hundred thousand yen, which was used to buy books, games, and a new television for young patients.",
      "However, the event had one problem that few visitors noticed. Every year, a number of ducks were caught by plants near the banks or carried past the finish line by the current, and some of them were never found. Two years ago, members of the volunteer club at Minamikawa High School counted the ducks after the race and discovered that eighteen of them were missing, which meant that eighteen pieces of plastic were left in the river.",
      "The students decided to solve this problem themselves. They first walked along the river and drew a map of the places where ducks were most likely to get stuck. ( A ), they asked the town office for permission to stretch a long net across the river just below the finish line, and they borrowed two small boats from a canoe club so that volunteers could check the edges of the water.",
      "On the day of the next race, about forty students wore bright vests and stood at the points marked on their map. As soon as the race ended, they began collecting ducks and checking each number on a list. It took almost two hours, and some students got very wet, but in the end every single duck was found and brought back to the bridge.",
      "The students were proud of the result, and the town has now asked the club to organize the collection every year. One member said that the project taught her an important lesson: a happy event should not leave problems behind for the river or for the people who live after us. Many visitors agree, and this year more ducks than ever were adopted.",
