    title: 'A Tongue Built Like a Brush',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "If you have ever been licked by a cat, you probably remember that its tongue felt rough, almost like sandpaper on your skin. A dog's tongue is soft and wet. Because of this, many people are surprised the first time a cat gives them a friendly lick. For a long time, people simply accepted this roughness as one of the strange facts about cats. In recent years, however, scientists have looked closely at the cat's tongue and found that it is a very clever tool.",
      "The surface of a cat's tongue is covered with hundreds of tiny hooks called papillae. They are made of keratin, the same hard material as our fingernails. They all point backward toward the throat. When a cat licks its fur, these hooks catch on knots and loose hairs and pull them out, much like the teeth of a comb. This is also why cats sometimes swallow so much hair that they later cough up a ball of it.",
      "The most surprising discovery came from a team of engineers in the United States around 2018. Using powerful cameras and scans, they found that each tiny hook has a small hollow shape at its tip, like a little scoop. ( A ), the hooks do much more than comb the fur. Each scoop picks up a little saliva and carries it deep into the coat, all the way down to the skin. The researchers suggested that this matters. A cat's fur is thick, and simply wetting the top layer would not clean it properly.",
      "Grooming in this way is important for a cat's health. Cats spend a large part of their waking time cleaning themselves, and some estimates say it may be as much as a quarter of the day. The saliva spread through the fur removes dirt and may help keep away small insects. It also helps cats stay cool. As the saliva dries, it takes heat away from the body, much as sweat does for humans. Big cats such as lions and tigers seem to have tongues of the same design, which suggests that it has worked well for a very long time.",
      "The engineers did not stop at studying cats. Inspired by the tongue, they made a plastic brush with soft hooks shaped like the papillae. They found that it was easy to clean, because the hair could be removed from the hooks with one simple stroke of the hand. Ideas like this may one day lead to better brushes for pets, or even to new ways of spreading liquids or medicine into thick, soft materials. The rough lick of a cat, it seems, is not just a strange feeling but a small lesson in good design.",
    ],
